"@value": "p. 50 (\"The dividing line between a controlled photomosaic and a photomap is not always easy to define since it rests primarily on the degree of cartographic enhancement of the photographic image. The photomosaic is normally a monochrome image and has some limited cartographic treatment usually--a title, grid, scale, some names, certain symbols. With the photomap, the cartographic effort is much greater--several colours may be used and a considerable amount of symbolized information added. However, this considerable effort is normally made only where the base is an orthophotographic one, in which case it becomes an orthophotomap.\")" } ] },  { "@id": "_:b8httpc0liddOtlocdOtgovauthoritiesgenreFormsgf2011026755", "@type": [ "http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#GenreFormElement" ], "http://www.loc.gov/mads/rdf/v1#elementValue": [ { "@language": "en",
